  "question": {
    "text": "The function \\(f(x)=\\sin x-k x-c\\), where \\(k\\) and \\(c\\) are constants, decreases always when",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "wJJ8e",
      "text": "\\(k > 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "kP0cg",
      "text": "\\(k \\geq 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ZIJ3k",
      "text": "\\(k  < 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "JwLAN",
      "text": "\\(k \\leq 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let \\(f(x)=\\sin x-k x-c\\) where \\(k\\) and \\(c\\) are constants. \\(f^{\\prime}(x)=\\cos x-k\\)\n\\(\\therefore f\\) decreases if \\(\\cos x \\leq k\\)\nThus, \\(f(x)=\\sin x-k x-c\\) decrease always when \\(k \\geq 1\\).",
    "image": null
  },
